{0 Testing}

A Silica app is tested at five levels. Most tests run on native OCaml, with no
browser:

{t
| Level | Tool | What it shows |
|-------|------|---------------|
| one machine | {!Silica.Machine.step} | a step gives the next state and the actions |
| every state | {!Check} | no state that the machine can reach breaks a property |
| the runtime | {!Silica_memory} | the components, timers, calls and history work together |
| the server | [Server.handle] | the HTTP policy, the handlers and the first HTML |
| the browser | jsdom and Playwright | the DOM renderer in a real page |
}

{1:machines Machines}

A machine is a value: start it, step it with events, and compare the snapshot,
the name of its state and the actions. The {{!page-tutorial}tutorial} and
{!page-machines.run} show it. This is the fastest test, and the right one for
the rules of one machine.

{1:checker The checker}

The checker runs every event in every state that it can reach, so one call
covers the paths that a list of examples would miss. Give it samples of the
server and the invariants of the app, and run it in a dune test. See
{!page-checker}.

{1:memory The memory platform}

{!Silica_memory} mounts a component with the same runtime as the browser, on
native OCaml. The test finds elements, presses them, types text, and answers
the Remote calls by hand. Nothing happens by itself: the clock moves only when
the test calls [advance].

The [Doc_hello] component calls the server when it starts. The test answers
the request:

{@ocaml[
# let page = Silica_memory.mount Doc_hello.component;;
val page : unit Silica_memory.t = <abstr>
# Silica_memory.html page;;
- : string = "<p>Loading.</p>"
# let request = List.hd (Silica_memory.pending page);;
val request : Silica_memory.request = <abstr>
# Silica_memory.path request, Silica_memory.body request;;
- : string * string = ("/api/greeting", "\"Ada\"")
# Silica_memory.reply request {|"Hello, Ada"|};;
- : unit = ()
# Silica_memory.html page;;
- : string = "<p>Hello, Ada</p>"
]}

A failed call and a retry:

{@ocaml[
# let page = Silica_memory.mount Doc_hello.component;;
val page : unit Silica_memory.t = <abstr>
# Silica_memory.network_error (List.hd (Silica_memory.pending page)) "down";;
- : unit = ()
# Silica_memory.press (Silica_memory.by_text page "Try again");;
- : unit = ()
# List.length (Silica_memory.requests page);;
- : int = 2
]}

Timers wait for the clock. The debounce of {!page-machines} settles 500
milliseconds after the last text:

{@ocaml[
# let page = Silica_memory.mount Doc_debounce.component;;
val page : unit Silica_memory.t = <abstr>
# let field =
    Silica_memory.by_role page Silica.Accessibility.Textbox ~name:"Word";;
val field : Silica_memory.element = <abstr>
# Silica_memory.input field "ocaml";;
- : unit = ()
# Silica_memory.advance page 499;;
- : unit = ()
# Silica_memory.text (Silica_memory.by_text page "Waiting for you to stop.");;
- : string = "Waiting for you to stop."
# Silica_memory.advance page 1;;
- : unit = ()
# Silica_memory.html page;;
- : string =
"<div><input aria-label=\"Word\" value=\"ocaml\" /><button>Clear</button><p>You typed ocaml.</p></div>"
]}

{2:roles Roles and names}

A test finds an element as a screen reader does: by its role and its
accessible name. {!Silica_memory.by_role} gives the one element that matches,
and also takes [~level], [~checked], [~selected] and [~expanded].
{!Silica.Accessibility} computes the role and the name from the view, with the
rules of WAI-ARIA for the HTML that Silica views use; its page lists them.
Hidden elements are left out. When no element or several match, the failure
lists the elements of that role, with their names and paths, so the fix is in
the message:

{@ocaml[
# let page = Silica_memory.mount Doc_debounce.component;;
val page : unit Silica_memory.t = <abstr>
# Silica_memory.by_role page Silica.Accessibility.Button ~name:"Clean";;
Exception:
Failure
 "Silica_memory: no button \"Clean\". The elements of role button are:\n  button \"Clear\" at div > button".
]}

{!Silica_memory.view_text} prints the view as {!Silica.View_text} does: one
line per element that has a role or a handler, with its name, its states and
its handlers. It is what a test, an agent or a reviewer reads to know what the
page shows, with no browser:

{@ocaml[
# Silica_memory.input
    (Silica_memory.by_role page Silica.Accessibility.Textbox ~name:"Word")
    "ocaml";;
- : unit = ()
# print_string (Silica_memory.view_text page);;
textbox "Word" value="ocaml" [input]
button "Clear" [press]
text "Waiting for you to stop."
- : unit = ()
]}

The value of a password field, an [<input type="password">], prints as
[value="••••••••"], eight bullets whatever its length, so that a typed secret
does not end in a golden file or a counterexample; an empty value prints as
[value=""] (see {!Silica.Secret}).

{2:props A root with props}

{!Silica_memory.mount_with_props} mounts a component that has props, and
{!Silica_memory.set_props} gives it new ones, as a parent does. The type of the
handle carries the type of the props:

{@ocaml[
# let page = Silica_memory.mount_with_props ~props:"Cups" Doc_stepper.component;;
val page : string Silica_memory.t = <abstr>
# Silica_memory.press
    (Silica_memory.by_role page Silica.Accessibility.Button ~name:"More Cups");;
- : unit = ()
# Silica_memory.set_props page "Mugs";;
- : unit = ()
# print_string (Silica_memory.view_text page);;
text "Mugs: 1"
button "Fewer Mugs" [press]
button "More Mugs" [press]
- : unit = ()
]}

{2:trace The transition log}

The memory platform records every start, step and end of every instance:
{!Silica_memory.trace} gives the run as a {!Silica.Trace.t}.
{!Silica.Trace.to_text} prints one line per record: its number, its time on the
memory clock, the instance path, the states before and after, how the step
went, the event and its cause, then the actions:

{@ocaml[
# let page = Silica_memory.mount Doc_debounce.component;;
val page : unit Silica_memory.t = <abstr>
# Silica_memory.input
    (Silica_memory.by_role page Silica.Accessibility.Textbox ~name:"Word")
    "ocaml";;
- : unit = ()
# Silica_memory.advance page 500;;
- : unit = ()
# print_string (Silica.Trace.to_text (Silica_memory.trace page));;
trace debounce / build memory from memory
1 +0ms debounce empty (started) <- start
2 +0ms debounce empty -> typing (entered) on input textbox "Word" <- input textbox "Word" (div > input) ocaml => start timer 1.1/debounce/typing/after 500ms 500ms
3 +500ms debounce typing -> settled (entered) on after 500ms <- timer 500ms 1.1/debounce/typing/after 500ms => stop 1.1/debounce/typing/after 500ms
- : unit = ()
]}

The cause says what made each step: the element of a handler (by role,
accessible name and path), a timer, the answer of a call, a Live update, a held
resource, the result of an action, new props, a child, a URL change or a message
of the host. An ignored event is recorded too, marked [unhandled].

{!Silica.Trace.to_jsonl} writes the trace as a file, JSON Lines: one header
line, then one {!Silica.Transition.t} per line; {!Silica.Trace.of_jsonl} reads
it back and names the line of an error. A record holds the event data, the
props and the snapshot only for machines whose codecs are registered with
{!Silica.Machine.register_codecs}; names, causes, answers and Live updates are
always there. The {!Silica.Trace} page says what a trace does not hold.

Text typed into a password field is masked in every trace, in the inspector
and in [silica inspect]: the input text of the cause is [••••••••], and in the
event data, props and snapshots each string that is the last text typed into a
password field is [••••••••] too (see {!Silica.Transition.observer}). A replay
of such a step in the check still runs, by the choice of the checker or by the
element of the cause; {!Silica_memory.val-replay} stops there, since it cannot
type the text again.

{!Silica_memory.val-replay} gives a fresh mount the inputs of a trace, such as a
counterexample of the checker or a recorded run, and says at which step the
state names differ, or why a step cannot run in memory (see
{!page-checker.replay}).

The role, name and path of the element of a handler are computed when the
trace is read, not when the input happens: the record keeps the element and
the elements above it, which are immutable data, so a press on a large page
costs the same with a recorder as without one.

The same records come from the browser and the server.
{!Silica_browser.recorder} makes a {!Silica.Trace.Recorder.t}, a ring of the
last transitions (1000 by default, names only unless [~data:true]); give it to
[Silica_browser.mount ~observe:(Trace.Recorder.observer recorder)] and send
[Trace.to_jsonl] through an endpoint of the app. [Server.live ~observe] and
[Server.create ~observe_live] give the transitions of a Live session to an
observer, such as [Silica.Trace.Recorder.observer]. With no observer nothing
is recorded, and a release bundle that does not make a recorder has no
recorder code.

A recorder without [~data:true] keeps names only ({!Silica.Trace.names_only}),
the default for production. Such a trace holds: the header (root machine,
build, platform, the path of the URL, the number of dropped records), and for
each record its number and time, the instance path (with the keys that the app
gave component nodes), the machine id, the state names before and after, the
change, the event name, the handler, role and element path of an element, the
delay of a timer, the endpoint path of a call, the topic of a Live update, the
name of a held resource and the actions. The ids of timers, calls and resources
are numbers, such as [21.2/comment-composer/#3]: the same id gets the same
number in one trace. It holds no event data, props, snapshots, answers, Live
updates, input text, accessible names, URL queries or fragments, page titles
or document attribute values, and no encoded input of a call (which the id of a
call holds with data). Key component nodes by ids, not by text that users type:
keys stay in the instance paths. A test in [test/conform] types two todos in
the example app and checks that the names-only trace holds neither. Names are
enough to compare a trace with the golden specs; to run the code and the
properties again on it, the app opts in to data, for example in development or
staging (see {!page-checker.conform}).

[mount ~answers] answers calls before the first paint, as a server render
does. {!Silica_memory.mount_app} runs a router app with a history: see
{!page-router.app}. The platform also records the actions, the running
resources, the title, the cookies, the log and the Live sockets, which the test
opens and feeds with messages.

What the memory platform does not do: it paints islands as empty elements,
it does not measure (a [Measure] gets {!Silica.Rect.zero}), and the resources
that need a layout, such as [Anchor] and [Scroll_lock], are only recorded.
Test those in a browser.

{1:server Server tests}

[Server.handle] is a function from a request to a response. With a
synchronous [Io], a test calls it directly: see {!page-server.try}. Test the
status of each check, the body of each handler, and the HTML of a page. For
Live sockets, [Server.live] runs one island with a [send] function that the
test gives, and [Server.receive] gives it a client message.

{1:browser Browser tests}

The repository has two sets of browser tests:

- [test/browser/]: Node tests over jsdom. They mount components with
  {!Silica_browser} and check the DOM, the hydration and the events. Run them
  with [dune build @test/browser/browser-test].
- [test/e2e/]: Playwright tests in Chromium, against the example server and the
  Silica site. They test what jsdom cannot: layout, focus, scroll, the history
  and the first paint with and without JavaScript. Run them with
  [npm --prefix test/e2e test].

A browser test waits for the attribute [data-silica-ready] on the root before
it acts: from then on the page reacts to input.
